// TASK-0298 cycle 143 reproducer schedule, now an in-tree
// integration-test target for the TASK-0324 same-set fan-out shape.
// LIFECYCLE:
//   - Pre-cycle-144: silent miscompile (transfer_inject elided the
//     cross-worker `tmp` transfers; runtime produced wrong vblur
//     output starting at row 2).
//   - Cycle 144 (AC#0/1/2/5): typed compile error
//     `TransferInjectError::SameSetSilentElisionRisk` replaced the
//     silent miscompile (diagnose-first guard
//     `check_no_silent_elision_risk`).
//   - Cycle 147 (AC#3/4): cross-worker codegen lands. The
//     validator's same-set rejection is lifted;
//     `build_waits_for_op`'s same-set short-circuit falls through
//     to the cartesian fan-out and emits N*(N-1) = 12 cross-worker
//     (src, dst) Push/Wait pairs for `tmp`. Bit-identical against
//     reference.bin on pthreads-sync + pthreads-async.
//
// MP-TCP COVERAGE (TASK-0327): mp-tcp-bufsync + mp-tcp-event still
// SKIP this schedule because their star topology has no worker-to-
// worker channel (the 12 cross-pairs fail LOUD with EmitError::
// ContractGap). That is a backend transport gap, NOT a transfer_inject
// gap — the pass-level emission is correct for all 4 backends, only
// the transport lowering fails.
//
// Both-passes-distributed schedule: pass 1 (hblur_acc) AND pass 2
// (vblur_acc) both placed on { w0..w3 } with partition=rows.
//
// TASK-0298 cycle 143 investigation: the existing distributed.sched.nuc
// places pass 2 on host (with N-to-1 gather of tmp) because pass 2's
// inner iv vm reads `tmp[vm][vx]` where vm sweeps 0..H regardless of
// the output (vy, vx) — every consumer worker needs the FULL tmp
// matrix. This schedule tests whether transfer_inject's broadcast
// machinery handles that shape: each producer worker w_i owns its
// hy row-band of tmp (from pass 1), and each consumer worker w_j
// needs the WHOLE tmp.
//
// Per-pair-tile shape (the question this schedule probes):
// - Pass 1: in_arr host -> {w0..w3}, row-banded by hy. Standard
//   1-to-N broadcast + per-tile slice. Already shipped in
//   distributed.sched.nuc.
// - Pass 2: tmp is produced by 4 workers (each its hy row-band)
//   and consumed by 4 workers (each its vy row-band, but the
//   inner vm sweep means EACH consumer needs ALL rows of tmp).
//   Conceptually: tmp = gather + broadcast (N-to-1-to-N). The
//   per-pair transfer (w_i -> w_j) is WHOLE-ARRAY on the consumer
//   side (vm covers 0..H) regardless of which output row band w_j
//   owns; only the producer side has a row-band slice.
//
// If transfer_inject's existing per-pair-tile machinery handles
// this (the N-to-M fan-out "Honest limitations" warning is about
// COORDINATE-MAPPING when both sides are partial, not when the
// consumer needs the whole thing), this schedule should lower
// cleanly bit-identical across all 4 tier-1 backends.
//
// If it fails (codegen error, wrong main.rs, or runtime data
// mismatch), the failure mode tells us exactly what gap to file.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    place load_image on host;
    place save_image on host;

    // Pass 1: distributed across 4 compute workers, row-banded by hy.
    // Identical to distributed.sched.nuc pass 1.
    place hblur_acc on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // Pass 2: ALSO distributed, row-banded by vy. (The shape under
    // investigation.)
    place vblur_acc on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // Row-band partition each pass's outer axis.
    loop hy : partition=rows;
    loop vy : partition=rows;

    // host -> workers: per-band slice of in_arr for pass 1.
    transfer in_arr : sync;

    // workers <-> workers: tmp transfer. The consumer side (vm
    // sweep) reads every row of tmp regardless of output row band,
    // so this is the broadcast-of-gather shape under test.
    transfer tmp : sync;

    // workers -> host: gather `out` from the 4 row-bands for save_image.
    // (Sibling of distributed.sched.nuc's `tmp` gather, just on `out`
    // because pass 2 is now on workers.)
    transfer out : sync;
}
